xual and  reproductive rights and health what it  what's parliament's position on this on  this i can continue just where i left  off the parliament's position is  extremely clear the rights have to be  enjoyed and promoted any everywhere and  that is the position that i will promote  as european parliament president that is  what i committed to and that is why  there are at the moment negotiations on  the so-called simon vale pact that seeks  to promote rights but also bring member  states together closer together um  keeping in mind member states competence  on this matter but on this my position  is clear my position is that is that of  the european parliament  more than half of eu cities since stated  in the in this public survey that they  want parliament to have a stronger role  in the eu  on this lian on instagram asked what can  be done to strengthen the parliament's  role on the eu  we have a golden opportunity now first  of all to transform into a modern  effective and efficient parliament we  have learned a lot during the pandemic  we are now looking into the  opportunities that we of what we learned  not only in using it in the way we work  here and the way we negotiate in the way  we communicate our decisions but also in  being a model for other parliaments  across the world  concretely we would like to do more in  terms of legislative initiative you know  there is a possibility for parliament  this parliament to initiate laws we we  know because we are the only directly  elected institution that there are gaps  in our legislative arsenal so to speak  what we can do citizens are asking us to  do more let's make sure we don't hide  behind political inability or sometimes  unwillingness by saying there is no  solution i am a fixer and i've always  wanted to find solutions to problems  which seem unsurmountable but we have  been here before there was a time when  this parliament was not a co-legislator  when this parliament used to simply give  consent sign on the dotted line whenever  there was a law that was adopted across  the road now we are co-legislators now  we have equality in the way we do laws  and i think that we need to make sure  that that role is pushed reinforced and  also delivers on what our citizens  want us to legislate upon and sometimes  you know there is a gap it's what we are  doing when we say it and what we do i  want to shorten that gap and be more  relevant  anna bondi from instagram asks what are  the three most important things we must  do and she puts in brackets besides your  vision i agree with her  to make europe more united eurovision is  important absolutely  totally agreed  in my country too  i think on climate we need to do more on  health with as we said we need to do  more but also i think we could be more  coherent and  consistent in foreign policy we have so  many  people messaging us from all over on on  facebook and on instagram telling us i  would like to hear a coherent voice i  live in a country which is worried about  a neighboring country that is not a  member of the european union and seeks  to trample on my rights seeks to  destabilize the union i think we can do  more and thanks to anna for for asking  the question on a foreign policy  perspective this european parliament has  long held very strong positions i think  it's about time also to align those  positions with the other institutions  because the competence is the same and  we can really deliver not only in  political uh in political statements but  tangible results in expressing  solidarity with those people  there is also a nice questions from  vicenzo from instagram and it says is it  still possible to make the dream of our  founding fathers come true namely of a  european of europe as an only a united  community of politics and defense  this is something that  occurs every time we discuss uh um  issues where we have failed where we  have succeeded and in reality he is  right vincenzo by saying listen  we have been talking about this for  decades  and we still have not delivered and  there are gaps uh in our possible in in  our capacity to act in a united manner  at the moment there are talks of the  so-called strategic autonomy what does  europe do where other parts of the world  don't act or if they act  how do we defend ourselves how do we  help those individual member states that  need solidarity here you have a delicate  path of analyzing different statuses of  different member states where they stand  but this european parliament has always  also been very clear in expressing  solidarity with those people who look to  europe  as they did to the founding fathers as  the beacon of hope prosperity democracy  but ultimately of rights  uh this same survey released today also  uh note that uh for your eu citizens  democracy is important but to protect  this democracy also as you said to  protect it from what's coming from  outside this information from the third  parties from actors uh uh foreign actors  uh is imminent in europe and it the eu  is fighting it  so  mark oliver asks what  should what is the what should the u.s  response be to to targeted campaigns  especially those funded by the by russia  for example disinformation and  misinformation by authoritarian regimes  is something that is becoming let's say  more tangible influencing  elections looking into spreading fake  root  and  even as we see  destabilizing  governments that are constructive in  order to try to emerge from this  pandemic we see also a need to counter  that narrative  at the extreme ends of the political  spectrum which try to misuse this  information again to destabilize or  spread fake news such as for example  rising energy prices are because of the  green deal proposals on the table which  is of course not a reality in this  parliament we have set up a committee  that is was set up precisely  to discuss uh and and how to combat this  misinformation and disinformation we  have been discussing this with tech  giants and social media operators and  network operators in order to see how to  protect the most vulnerable members of  our society especially our children who  are you know who have sometimes been  seen as victims of such of such misuse  of data and misinformation the report  and i look very much forward to reading  it is presented to our parliament in  march and then we will take the steps  forward from there  you have already mentioned that we need  to bring europe closer to towns and to  villages across europe and romaine from  instagram would like to know how to  reconnect european institutions to  citizens  if i can tell you what my favorite part  of being an mvp a member of the european  parliament is is to enter a school  and discuss with children of all ages  why  they should believe in politics  why politics is not a bad word why  if you vote it makes a difference why  because if you don't vote people who you  might not feel represented by will fill  that gap and take decisions that you  don't like or are not good for you  the  reaction of going and doing that not  only in your member state but in each  and every part of the european union and  i had already started doing that when i  was vice president and i will definitely  continue to do that now as president is  priceless because they get to see that  european union is not only about what  happens in this building it's not only  about what happens in our building in  strasbourg or also in luxembourg where  we also have  have um  workforce people who work for the  european parliament  who who live and work in luxembourg but  but that you go out of these cities into  the member states and say i'm here ask  me what you want and i'm here to deliver  and it could be the most rural  far away par part from the capital uh of  of a country it is so important that the  european union is present and the  european parliament represents everybody  living on our territory  actually on this we have a question as  well by and sirica from instagram and  she wonders how difficult is to succeed  in in politics as a woman  i think  i have to thank  women who came before me  for having worked so hard  to break those feelings those barriers  in order for me and my colleagues in  this house  40 members are women we need to have  more but if you look at the current  composition of the bureau the vice  presidents more women than men so  different scenario to what has been for  all these decades  it is  us that we need to thank  uh for the example for the effort of  those women who came before us and on  whose shoulders as i said in my in my  speech when i got elected we stand with  pride with honor but with an obligation  to make sure that we pass on  this overcome challenge still many  challenges to go to the women and girls  who come after us  for those who for doing those young  girls girls who are watching us now what  would be the advice you could give them  right now  make sure your voice is heard  never wait for somebody else to talk for  you you have a voice that can be as loud  and the integrity of your argument can  matter irrespective of where you come  from  where you live which country you are  currently in you could be traveling you  could be working you could be  visiting another member state but you  could be in your house today watching  after you've come back from school after  you've come back from work or if you're  still at work and connected to the  european parliament to see what you are  doing make sure you make your voice  heard  for  uh many people  maybe do not know that you are married  to a finn so your family is truly  european from south to north absolutely  but  there must be some cultural differences  different or that one culture difference  that you still find odd even after these  years you know we say united in  diversity i think that is very good  statement to  to  explain my marriage i met my husband  from finland when we were in youth  politics  when we were doing i was doing actually  a lot of the same things i'm doing now  uh in in seeking  a role of convincing people to vote  and engage themselves from the ground in  a campaign  cultural difference  linguistic there is for sure  i would say perhaps not the sauna you  know this is a very specific finnish  nordic but mostly finnish  tradition which my husband engages in  very actively  i have learned to get accustomed to that  but they also have this very weird thing  of digging a hole in the ice and jumping  into it i'm never going to do that not  for a mediterranean  never  and maybe before we wrap up there is a  question from liz lindberg on linkedin  she asks how young people can contribute  or engage in the future of the european  union  they have the opportunity to do that now  it's not only about voting we are  currently engaged in the discussions on  the conference on the future of europe  big panels given and dedicated to young  people in most in a lot of member states  whether it is online a lot of pandas  have taken place online but in a lot of  countries they are being they are taking  place with big communication  possibilities uh in presence  send your messages we will listen to  them it's our responsibility because in  2024 i need to convince together with my  colleagues here that this place is worth  keeping because we work for you  and thanks for mentioning the conference  on the future of europe which is still  uh ongoing and ideas are taking shape  now and will be soon presented to the  european parliament so hopefully you  will keep them coming exactly keep them  coming and you have also promised that  you will come back to our uh  live chats yes uh so we hope to have you  back soon thank you so much looking  forward thank you and thank you to  everyone who has uh followed this live  stream  thank you so much president metzolia and  thank you everyone for tuning in thank  you have a good afternoon bye  you
 

